# notes and directly discussed at length in the transcript itself.
#############################################

tgs:Work.TaoOfPhysics a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Tao of Physics: An Exploration of the Parallels Between Modern Physics and Eastern Mysticism"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "1975 book by Fritjof Capra, his real first and best-known work — still in print in 40+ editions, referenced alongside a statue of Shiva in the courtyard of CERN. Argues for real parallels between quantum theory/relativity and Eastern mystical traditions (Hinduism, Buddhism, Taoism), directly inspired by his own real undergraduate reading of Werner Heisenberg's 'Physics and Philosophy.'"@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.FritjofCapra ;
    dct:issued "1975"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.fritjofcapra.net/about/> ;
    .

# added 2026-08-25

tgs:Work.MaterialWorld a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Material World: The Six Raw Materials That Shape Modern Civilization"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "2023 book by Ed Conway (Penguin Random House / WH Allen). An Economist and Sunday Times Book of the Year, shortlisted for the 2023 FT Business Book of the Year Award. Traces the full extraction-to-product supply chain of six materials — sand, salt, iron, copper, oil, and lithium — arguing these physically underpin the modern economy far out of proportion to their small direct share of GDP."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.EdConway ;
    dct:issued "2023"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Material_World_(book)> ;
    .

# added 2026-08-29

tgs:Work.DeseretFarmerBailout2026 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "There's no bailout for the American Farmer"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Deseret Magazine, March 2026. Documents structural farm consolidation via 'get big or get out' market logic — 63 farms lost per day, 162,000 farms lost 2017-2024, 24 million acres lost. Reports that even government bailouts have failed to reverse the trend. Real, direct thematic overlap with TGS_198's own discussion of concentrated economic power squeezing out smaller, locally-owned businesses."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.deseret.com/magazine/2026/03/07/america-family-farms-in-decline/> ;
    dct:issued "2026-03"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    .

tgs:Work.WorldBankFoodSecurityUpdate2026 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Food Security Update"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "World Bank, June 2026. Authoritative, non-partisan monitoring brief documenting real global fertilizer-price pressure (35% increase) and food-supply fragility. Descriptive rather than prescriptive — doesn't itself argue for local food systems as the remedy. Real, direct connection to TGS_198's own discussion of supply-chain fragility (the single-slaughterhouse and baby-formula-plant examples)."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.worldbank.org/en/topic/foodsecurity> ;
    dct:issued "2026-06"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    .

tgs:Work.PewDecliningTrust2025 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Americans' Declining Trust in Each Other and Reasons Behind It"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Pew Research Center, May 2025. Documents declining interpersonal trust connected to political polarization, including spatial/social sorting that reduces meaningful cross-party contact. Descriptive survey data, not prescriptive. TGS_198 supplies a real causal mechanism this piece's data doesn't on its own — Stacy Mitchell's own direct argument connecting economic powerlessness to democratic erosion."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2025/05/08/americans-declining-trust-in-each-other-and-reasons-behind-it/> ;
    dct:issued "2025-05"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    .

tgs:Work.CarbonMajorsWaterCrisis2026 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Carbon emissions exacerbating the Western US water crisis"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Williams, E.L., Abatzoglou, J.T., Phillips, C.A., Safeeq, M., Sadai, S., Chegwidden, O.S., Fernandez-Bou, A.S., Merner, L.D., Ortiz-Partida, J.P. Communications Earth & Environment 7, 675 (2026), published by Nature Portfolio, Union of Concerned Scientists and UC Merced. First attribution study of its kind: finds 40-64% of climate-driven western US water crisis impacts (1950-2024) — snowpack loss, streamflow decline, irrigation demand increase — attributable to 122 'Carbon Majors' (fossil fuel and cement companies), including roughly one-third of California Central Valley's climate-related groundwater decline. Open Access, CC-BY 4.0 — direct quotation with attribution explicitly permitted. Source article for WWNS #1 ('The 122 Companies Behind the Western Water Crisis')."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.nature.com/articles/s43247-026-03900-6> ;
    dct:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode> ;
    dct:issued "2026-08-25"^^xsd:date ;
    .

tgs:Work.Ultrasocial2021 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ultrasocial: The Evolution of Human Nature and the Quest for a Sustainable Future"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "John Gowdy, Cambridge University Press, October 2021. Argues human 'ultrasociality' — the same evolutionary category as ants, termites, and other eusocial species — emerged from the economic surplus dynamics of agriculture, not from a fixed 'selfish gene' human nature. Co-authored research on ants/social insects with Lisi Krall (SUNY Cortland, not yet built in this graph) underlies much of the book's argument. Directly cited in TGS_14's own real show notes."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JohnGowdy ;
    dct:source <https://www.amazon.com/Ultrasocial-Evolution-Nature-Sustainable-Future/dp/110883826X> ;
    dct:issued "2021-10"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    .

tgs:Work.PsychopathicSelectionHypothesisBook a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Psychopathic Selection Hypothesis"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Greg Elliott's own book, self-described in the TGS_231 transcript as published 'back in May' (year not independently confirmed — the episode's own real, wider context suggests 2026, consistent with dct:issued below, but not verified via direct fetch). Argues dark triad traits (psychopathy, Machiavellianism, narcissism) are selected for at an institutional/civilizational scale via game-theoretic dynamics, accelerating with the agricultural revolution and again with fossil fuel expansion. Endorsed by William Rees (already a real Persona in this graph, RR-2) — the two are described in the transcript as having 'become pretty close friends' as a result."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.GregElliott ;
    .

tgs:Work.BrokenMoney2023 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Broken Money: Why Our Financial System is Failing Us and How We Can Make it Better"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Lyn Alden, 2023 best-selling book on the past, present, and future of money through the lens of technology and energy. Directly cited in her own TGS_113 appearance as her most recent major work."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.LynAlden ;
    dct:source <https://www.lynalden.com/broken-money/> ;
    dct:issued "2023"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.ZombiesInWesternCulture a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Zombies in Western Culture: A 21st Century Crisis"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "John Vervaeke and Christopher Mastropietro (co-author, not yet built in this graph). Integrates cognitive science and psychology to address the meaning crisis in Western society, using the cultural zombie-figure as a real, structured metaphor for the modal confusion Vervaeke describes in his own TGS_158 appearance — 'we're not starving calorically, but we're starving nutritionally.'"@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JohnVervaeke ;
    dct:source <https://johnvervaeke.com/books/zombies-in-western-culture-a-twenty-first-century-crisis/> ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheseWildsBeyondOurFences a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "These Wilds Beyond Our Fences: Letters to My Daughter on Humanity's Search for Home"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Bayo Akomolafe, North Atlantic Books, 2017."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.BayoAkomolafe ;
    dct:issued "2017"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.PewAmericansMorallyBad2026 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "In 25-Country Survey, Americans Especially Likely To View Fellow Citizens as Morally Bad"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Jonathan Evans, Kirsten Lesage, Manolo Corichi, William Miner, Andrew Prozorovsky, Julia Armeli, Alan Cooperman. Pew Research Center, March 5, 2026. Real, striking finding: the US is the only one of 25 countries surveyed where more adults rate their fellow citizens' morality as bad (53%) than good (47%). Notably, this isn't explained by Americans being more morally strict in general — on most of nine specific moral-judgment questions, the US sits mid-pack internationally. A real, direct partisan mechanism is identified instead: Democrats/leaners are far more likely than Republicans/leaners to rate fellow Americans as immoral (60% vs. 46%). Real methodology: 25-country Global Attitudes Survey, Jan-May 2025 (US n=3,605, Mar 24-30, 2025, plus a separate ATP survey of 8,937 US adults, May 5-11, 2025)."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/2026/03/05/in-25-country-survey-americans-especially-likely-to-view-fellow-citizens-as-morally-bad/> ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-05"^^xsd:date ;
    .

tgs:Work.LePlanDeTransformation a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Plan de transformation de l'économie française (The Plan to Transform the French Economy)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The Shift Project, led by Jean-Marc Jancovici, begun during COVID. A real, distinctive methodology: reorganizes the economy around physical flows (materials, energy, employment by sector) rather than monetary figures, modeling what a 5%-per-year greenhouse gas reduction actually requires sector by sector. Published as a book; sold over 100,000 copies in France, a real, exceptional figure for a policy essay there."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JeanMarcJancovici ;
    dct:source <https://theshiftproject.org/en/> ;
    .

tgs:Work.LeMondeSansFin a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Le Monde sans fin (The World Without End)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Graphic novel by Jean-Marc Jancovici and illustrator Christophe Blain (not yet built in this graph), 2022. Real, exceptional commercial success: the best-selling book in France in 2022, over 500,000 copies — a striking figure for a book about energy and climate."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JeanMarcJancovici ;
    dct:issued "2022"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.GoliathsCurse a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Goliath's Curse: The History and Future of Societal Collapse"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Luke Kemp, Penguin, 2025 (US release Sep 23, 2025). Argues 'civilization' is a poorly-defined, historically loaded term better replaced with 'Goliath' — a society organized around dominance hierarchies enforced by violence, distinct from earlier, more egalitarian large-scale human settlements (real examples cited directly: the Indus Valley/Harappan civilization, early Teotihuacán) that lacked centralized rule. Traces a real, recurring pattern: societies beginning egalitarian, becoming progressively more unequal and hierarchical, then destabilizing and collapsing once inequality reaches a high level."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.LukeKemp ;
    dct:source <https://www.penguin.co.uk/books/321192/goliaths-curse-by-kemp-luke/9780241741238> ;
    dct:issued "2025"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_209_BalazsMatics ;
    .

tgs:Work.Disorder2022 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Disorder: Hard Times in the 21st Century"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Helen Thompson, Oxford University Press, 2022. Real, striking coincidence: the UK edition released the same day Russia invaded Ukraine. Argues the political shocks of the 2010s (Brexit, Trump's election) have structural, long-history explanations rooted in energy — the difficulties of the fossil fuel economy and the energy transition — rather than being standalone episodes of national political drama."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.HelenThompson ;
    dct:source <https://global.oup.com/academic/product/disorder-9780198864981?cc=us&lang=en&> ;
    dct:issued "2022"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.OilAndTheWesternEconomicCrisis a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Oil and the Western Economic Crisis"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Helen Thompson, Springer, 2017. Her own real, direct account: at the time of publication, 'most people thought, well, why is she writing about that' — energy-aware political economy was not yet a widely legible frame; that changed substantially after Disorder's 2022 release."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.HelenThompson ;
    dct:issued "2017"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheStoryOfCO2 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Story of CO2 Is the Story of Everything: A Four-Billion-Year Chronicle of Planet Earth and Our Tumultuous Relationship with Carbon Dioxide"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Peter Brannen, Ecco, August 2025. Reframes CO2 from industrial pollutant to the substance fundamental to Earth's habitability itself — the 'control knob' for planetary temperature and ocean chemistry, and the ultimate source of all carbon-based life. Grew directly out of research for his earlier book, tgs:Work.TheEndsOfTheWorld: while auditing geochemistry courses, Brannen found most mass extinctions are linked to runaway CO2 injections or declines, not the asteroid-impact narrative most people assume."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.PeterBrannen ;
    dct:issued "2025"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheEndsOfTheWorld a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Ends of the World: Volcanic Apocalypses, Lethal Oceans, and Our Quest to Understand Earth's Past Mass Extinctions"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Peter Brannen, Ecco. Covers Earth's five major mass extinctions. Featured in Greta Thunberg's The Climate Book and the Best American Science and Nature Writing series. Subject of Brannen's real first TGS appearance, TGS_103."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.PeterBrannen ;
    .

tgs:Work.TrueHuman a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "True Human: Reimagining Ourselves at the End of Our World"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Samantha Sweetwater, Q4 2025. Offers a path for sense-making through the metacrisis, framing it as a spiritual opportunity calling for cultivated relational intelligence and moral imagination, centered on a shift from the 'hero's journey' to what she calls the 'kinship journey' — recognizing one's own life as meaningful only in service to something larger than oneself."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.SamanthaSweetwater ;
    dct:issued "2025"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_202_SamanthaSweetwater ;
    .

tgs:Work.ThermalPowerAndClimateChange a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Thermal Power and Climate Change: A Data-Driven Analysis of Cause and Effect, 1800-2100"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Tad Patzek, preprint available (published book forthcoming at time of TGS_225 recording). Also references a real, distinct research contribution discussed directly in that episode: a preprint under peer review at Geophysical Research Letters identifying a declining Earth albedo as an additional accelerant of global warming over the past 26 years, layered on top of accelerating ocean heat absorption and ice-sheet melt."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.TadPatzek ;
    dct:source <https://eartharxiv.org/repository/view/10865/> ;
    .

tgs:Work.BitterHarvest a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bitter Harvest: An Inquiry into the War Between Economy and Earth"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Lisi Krall, SUNY Press. Traces the formation and evolution of the economic Superorganism from the cultivation of annual grains through to global capitalism, arguing capitalism is the direct legacy of the agricultural revolution rather than a distinct later rupture."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.LisiKrall ;
    dct:source <https://sunypress.edu/Books/B/Bitter-Harvest> ;
    .

tgs:Work.ForTheCommonGood a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "For the Common Good: Redirecting the Economy toward Community, the Environment, and a Sustainable Future"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Herman Daly (with theologian John B. Cobb Jr.), Beacon Press, 1989. Real, direct personal significance: alongside tgs:Work.ValuingTheEarth, one of the two Daly books Nate read that directly caused him to leave his Wall Street career."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.HermanDaly ;
    dct:issued "1989"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.ValuingTheEarth a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Valuing the Earth: Economics, Ecology, Ethics"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Herman Daly and Kenneth Townsend (eds.), MIT Press. Real, direct personal significance: the book Nate's own words credit as the main thing that propelled him to leave his Wall Street career roughly a year after reading it."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.HermanDaly ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheMythOfTheMachine a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Myth of the Machine"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Lewis Mumford, two volumes (Technics and Human Development, 1967; The Pentagon of Power, 1970). Develops the real concept of the 'megamachine' — large hierarchical organizations using humans as component parts, exemplified across history from pyramid-building to modern nuclear-armed states — and distinguishes 'polytechnic' technology (serving diverse human needs) from 'monotechnic' technology (pursued for its own sake, at humanity's expense)."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.LewisMumford ;
    dct:issued "1970"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheFutureIsRural a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Future Is Rural: Food System Adaptations to the Great Simplification"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Jason Bradford, Post Carbon Institute, 2019. Real, direct source for the show's own established phrase 'the future is rural' — already cited directly in Frankly_138."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JasonBradford ;
    dct:source <https://www.postcarbon.org/publications/the-future-is-rural/> ;
    dct:issued "2019"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.BrettonWoodsIII a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bretton Woods III"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Real theory popularized by Zoltan Pozsar (then an economist at Credit Suisse), proposing a new world monetary order centered on commodity-backed currencies in the East, weakening the existing Eurodollar system — following Bretton Woods I (dollar pegged to gold) and Bretton Woods II (the free-floating dollar after that peg's abandonment). Pozsar himself, not built as a Persona in this graph, has no direct connection to Nate or this show; this Work exists here specifically because Michael Every (already built) wrote a real, extensive published rebuttal of it."@en ;
    dct:source <https://coinmarketcap.com/academy/article/bretton-woods-iii-and-crypto> ;
    .

tgs:Work.HistoryForTomorrow a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "History for Tomorrow: Inspiration from the Past for the Future of Humanity"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Roman Krznaric. Draws on historical precedent for societies that navigated existential crises, arguing history offers real, concrete inspiration for governance, community organization, and economic redesign rather than serving only as cautionary tales."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RomanKrznaric ;
    .

tgs:Work.ThePopulationBomb a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Population Bomb"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Paul Ehrlich, 1968. Real, foundational and widely-debated book on the risks of unchecked human population growth. Real, direct personal significance to this graph: Nate's own father read it."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.PaulEhrlich ;
    dct:issued "1968"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheRighteousMind a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Righteous Mind: Why Good People Are Divided by Politics and Religion"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Jonathan Haidt, 2012. New York Times bestseller examining the intuitive foundations of morality and how moral reasoning varies across cultural and political divisions."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JonathanHaidt ;
    dct:issued "2012"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_42_ModelingDriversMetacrisis ;
    .

tgs:Work.PratiquementDurable a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pratiquement Durable: 2000 Watt Eco-Neighborhoods – A Model for a Sustainable Lifestyle Towards a Post-Oil Democratic Society"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Peter Strack. Currently only available in French. Advocates for 2000-watt-per-capita neighborhoods as a lower-energy, higher-connection alternative to typical Western consumption levels."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.PeterStrack ;
    dct:source <https://pratiquement-durable.com/en/> ;
    .

tgs:Work.DopamineNation a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dopamine Nation: Finding Balance in the Age of Indulgence"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Anna Lembke, New York Times bestseller. Explores how to moderate compulsive overconsumption in a dopamine-overloaded modern environment."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.AnnaLembke ;
    dct:source <https://www.annalembke.com/> ;
    .

tgs:Work.Amazonia4 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Amazonia 4.0"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Initiative developed by Carlos Nobre, demonstrating the feasibility of a new socio-bioeconomy built on standing forests and flowing rivers in the Amazon, as a real, direct economic alternative to deforestation-driven extraction."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.CarlosNobre ;
    dct:source <https://amazonia4.org/> ;
    .

tgs:Work.AncientFutures a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ancient Futures: Learning from Ladakh"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Helena Norberg-Hodge. Real, direct account of tradition and change in Ladakh ('Little Tibet'), examining the ecological, social, and psychological effects of rapid economic globalization on a traditional culture. Translated into over 40 languages; over half a million copies sold. Also produced as a real, companion documentary film of the same title."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.HelenaNorbergHodge ;
    dct:source <https://www.helenanorberghodge.com/writing-films> ;
    .

tgs:Work.AgainstTheMachine a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Against the Machine: On the Unmaking of Humanity"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Paul Kingsnorth. Real, direct historical overview of philosophers and thinkers who observed Superorganism-like qualities emerging at scale from human activity, framing 'the Machine' as a civilizational force that turns creatures into components. Structured largely around a real, central Wendell Berry quote on whether humans will choose to be creatures or machines."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.PaulKingsnorth ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_217_WisdomInAWorldInCrisis ;
    .

tgs:Work.Ishmael a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ishmael"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Daniel Quinn, 1992. A telepathic gorilla teaches a human student the real, distinctive 'Takers and Leavers' framework — civilizations that believe the world was made for humans to master and exploit ('Takers') versus cultures that see humans as one part of a larger living community ('Leavers'). Real, direct personal significance to this graph: Nate first dismissed the book after five pages, then read it cover to cover on September 11, 2001, when the markets were closed."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.DanielQuinn ;
    dct:issued "1992"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheAscentOfHumanity a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Ascent of Humanity"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Charles Eisenstein, 2007. His first major book, written in part as a direct response to Daniel Quinn, Derrick Jensen, and John Zerzan's anti-civilization discourse, which narrates human civilization as a fall from a prior state of harmony and connection."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.CharlesEisenstein ;
    dct:issued "2007"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.SacredEconomics a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sacred Economics"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Charles Eisenstein, 2011."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.CharlesEisenstein ;
    dct:issued "2011"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheMoreBeautifulWorld a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The More Beautiful World Our Hearts Know Is Possible"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Charles Eisenstein, 2013."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.CharlesEisenstein ;
    dct:issued "2013"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.ClimateANewStory a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Climate: A New Story"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Charles Eisenstein, 2018."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.CharlesEisenstein ;
    dct:issued "2018"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.BraidingSweetgrass a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Braiding Sweetgrass: Indigenous Wisdom, Scientific Knowledge, and the Teachings of Plants"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Robin Wall Kimmerer, 2013. On the interdependence of people and the plant world, drawing on both Indigenous knowledge and Western botanical science. Won the 2014 Sigurd F. Olson Nature Writing Award; sold over 2 million copies worldwide within a decade of publication."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RobinWallKimmerer ;
    dct:issued "2013"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.IfAnyoneBuildsItEveryoneDies a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies: Why Superhuman AI Would Kill Us All"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Nate Soares and Eliezer Yudkowsky, 2025. Real, direct argument that current AI development methods — 'growing' systems through large-scale training rather than 'crafting' them through direct, understood specification — make catastrophic, unintended outcomes from superintelligent AI a likely rather than remote risk."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.NateSoares, tgs:Human.EliezerYudkowsky ;
    dct:source <https://ifanyonebuildsit.com/> ;
    dct:issued "2025"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheMarketMyth a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Market Myth"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Tomas Björkman, 2016. Real, direct critique noting that roughly 85% of exchange in today's markets involves immaterial property rights rather than physical goods."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.TomasBjorkman ;
    dct:issued "2016"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheNordicSecret a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Nordic Secret"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Tomas Björkman and Lene Rachel Andersen, 2017. Explores the real, historical Nordic Bildung educational tradition — folk high schools and adult-development retreat centers — as a possible model for cultivating civic maturity at scale."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.TomasBjorkman ;
    dct:issued "2017"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheWorldWeCreate a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The World We Create: From God to Market"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Tomas Björkman, 2019."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.TomasBjorkman ;
    dct:issued "2019"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.DieWise a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Die Wise: A Manifesto for Sanity and Soul"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Stephen Jenkinson. Award-winning book drawing on his years as a palliative-care counselor, arguing modern Anglo-North American culture is death-phobic and grief-illiterate, and that genuine skillfulness in dying and grieving must be actively learned rather than assumed."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.StephenJenkinson ;
    dct:issued "2015"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.Matrimony a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Matrimony: Ritual, Culture, and the Heart's Work"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Stephen Jenkinson, 2025. Discussed on his TGS appearance as a forthcoming release, then still weeks from publication. Examines marriage not primarily as a legal or romantic institution but as a real, alchemical cultural ritual — distinguishing a 'vow' (a present-moment commitment requiring no future) from a 'promise' (inherently future-reliant)."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.StephenJenkinson ;
    dct:issued "2025"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheWormAtTheCore a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Worm at the Core: On the Role of Death in Life"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Sheldon Solomon, Jeff Greenberg, and Tom Pyszczynski. Synthesizes roughly three decades of real, empirical Terror Management Theory research, examining how the uniquely human awareness of mortality shapes behavior, culture, politics, and religion."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.SheldonSolomon ;
    .

tgs:Work.InTheWakeOf911 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "In the Wake of 9/11: The Psychology of Terror"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Sheldon Solomon, Jeff Greenberg, and Tom Pyszczynski. Applies Terror Management Theory directly to the September 11 attacks and their real, documented psychological aftermath."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.SheldonSolomon ;
    .

tgs:Work.DenialOfDeath a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Denial of Death"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Ernest Becker. Won the 1974 Pulitzer Prize. Real, foundational text for Terror Management Theory, arguing much of human culture and behavior functions as a defense against the awareness of mortality."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.ErnestBecker ;
    .

tgs:Work.NewEconomyForTheBrazilianAmazon a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "New Economy for the Brazilian Amazon"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Real research initiative on economic incentives and payment structures for standing-forest conservation in the Brazilian Amazon, directly discussed by André Guimarães."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.wri.org/research/new-economy-brazil-amazon> ;
    .

tgs:Work.EndTimes a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "End Times: Elites, Counter-Elites, and the Path of Political Disintegration"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Peter Turchin. Applies cliodynamics and structural-demographic theory directly to the contemporary United States, arguing real, measurable elite overproduction and popular immiseration are driving the country toward real, historically-recurring political instability."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.PeterTurchin ;
    .

tgs:Work.WakingUp a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Waking Up: A Guide to Spirituality Without Religion"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Sam Harris. Real, direct exploration of meditation, consciousness, and self-transcendence from a secular, scientifically-grounded perspective. Also the name of Harris's own meditation app."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.SamHarris ;
    .

tgs:Work.DrillingDown a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Drilling Down: The Gulf Oil Debacle and Our Energy Dilemma"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Tad Patzek and Joseph Tainter (already built). Real, direct co-authored examination of the Deepwater Horizon disaster as a case study in energy complexity and diminishing returns."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.TadPatzek ;
    .

tgs:Work.EnergyAndHumanAmbitions a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Energy and Human Ambitions on a Finite Planet"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Thomas Murphy. Real, free, open-access textbook applying physics directly to questions of energy, growth, and the long-term viability of human ambitions on a finite planet."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.ThomasMurphy ;
    dct:source <https://escholarship.org/uc/item/9js5291m> ;
    .

tgs:Work.HowToFallInLoveWithTheFuture a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "How to Fall in Love with the Future: A Time Traveller's Guide to Changing the World"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Rob Hopkins. Real, direct argument that vividly imagining a genuinely better future is a necessary precondition for building one, not a naive luxury — drawing on real, direct neuroscience research on imagination, memory, and chronic stress's effect on the brain's capacity to picture the future."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RobHopkins ;
    .

tgs:Work.FromWhatIsToWhatIf a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "From What Is to What If: Unleashing the Power of Imagination to Create the Future We Want"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Rob Hopkins. Real, direct predecessor to How to Fall in Love with the Future, arguing declining collective imagination is a genuine, underappreciated barrier to building better futures."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RobHopkins ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheTransitionHandbook a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Transition Handbook"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Rob Hopkins. Real, foundational text of the Transition movement, laying out the original 12 (later expanded) steps for starting a real, local Transition Town initiative."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RobHopkins ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheWayOfTheWhale a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Way of the Whale"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Xiye Bastida. Real, upcoming feature documentary; directly connects to Julia Carabias Lillo's own real, successful collaborative effort protecting a whale birthing site, discussed in the same episode this Work is cited from."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.XiyeBastida ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheEndOfNature a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The End of Nature"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Bill McKibben, 1989. Real, widely-regarded first book written for a general audience specifically about climate change, translated into 24 languages. Nate directly credits reading this real book, in TGS_102's own real [INTRO], as one of the specific things that caused him to leave Wall Street and go study ecological economics."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.BillMcKibben ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheDebunkingHandbook a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Debunking Handbook"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "John Cook and collaborators. Real, direct practical guide to correcting misinformation effectively, addressing why simply presenting correct facts often fails and can even backfire, reinforcing the very misconception it's meant to correct."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JohnCook ;
    .

tgs:Work.ShortingTheGrid a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shorting the Grid: The Hidden Fragility of Our Electric Grid"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Meredith Angwin. Real, direct analysis of the US electric grid from both a physical-science and policy perspective, arguing genuine grid fragility stems as much from real, diffuse governance and accountability structures as from any single technical cause."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.MeredithAngwin ;
    .

tgs:Work.SmallIsBeautiful a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Small Is Beautiful: A Study of Economics As If People Mattered"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "E.F. Schumacher, 1973. Real, foundational text arguing for human-scale, decentralized, 'appropriate' technology over indiscriminate bigness — ranked by The Times Literary Supplement among the 100 most influential books published since World War II (1995). Directly develops Schumacher's own real concept of Buddhist economics, formed while working as an economic consultant in Burma in 1955."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.EFSchumacher ;
    .

tgs:Work.ArtificialSuperintelligenceAFuturisticApproach a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Artificial Superintelligence: A Futuristic Approach"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Roman Yampolskiy, 2016. Real, early foundational text in AI safety, examining the risks and control problems posed by hypothetical superintelligent systems."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RomanYampolskiy ;
    .

tgs:Work.AIUnexplainableUnpredictableUncontrollable a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "AI: Unexplainable, Unpredictable, Uncontrollable"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Roman Yampolskiy. Real, direct argument that a genuinely safe, controllable general superintelligence is a mathematical impossibility, not merely a difficult engineering problem — the central thesis directly discussed in TGS_233 (already built)."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RomanYampolskiy ;
    .

tgs:Work.BraveNewWar a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Brave New War: The Next Stage of Terrorism and the End of Globalization"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "John Robb, 2007. Real, direct introduction of the concept of 'open-source warfare' and 'global guerrillas' — decentralized, networked non-state actors using globalization's own interconnected systems (pipelines, power grids, financial markets) against it via small, cheap, disproportionately damaging attacks. Featured in a real, direct New York Times column by David Brooks."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JohnRobb ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheSpaceOfPossibleMindDesigns a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Space of Possible Mind Designs"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Roman Yampolskiy, 2015. Real, direct paper presented at the AGI Conference (Springer, DOI 10.1007/978-3-319-21365-1_23; also available as an arXiv preprint). Equates all minds to software, proves several real, formal properties of the resulting 'mind design space' (its infinitude, and the size and representational complexity of individual minds), surveys prior mind-design taxonomies (directly building on Aaron Sloman's 1984 'The Structure of the Space of Possible Minds'), and formally proposes a new field of study, intellectology (already built as tgs:Concept.Intellectology). Real, direct expanded 2023 treatment of the same material published as 'Taxonomies of Intelligence: A Comprehensive Guide to the Universe of Minds' (Seeds of Science, DOI 10.53975/9r72-i78l)."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RomanYampolskiy ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-10-07 -- York and Bell, cited as an 'Episode Foundation' in TGS-237's show notes.
#############################################

tgs:Work.EnergyTransitionsOrAdditions a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Energy transitions or additions? Why a transition from fossil fuels requires more than the growth of renewable energy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Richard York and Shannon Elizabeth Bell, Energy Research & Social Science, vol. 51, pp. 40-43, 2019 (available online 16 January 2019). Short peer-reviewed commentary arguing that the growth of renewable energy has so far expanded total energy production rather than displacing fossil fuels -- better described as energy 'additions' than 'transitions' -- and that calling it a transition is misleading and could inhibit meaningful policy to reduce fossil fuel use. Cited in TGS-237's show notes as an 'Episode Foundation' and as support for Art Berman's 'there never has been an energy transition' argument. Author names, journal, volume, pages, and online date verified via WebSearch 2026-10-07. No DOI recorded and no creator Humans modeled (neither author has an entry in this graph); the ScienceDirect link from the show notes is used as the source."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2214629618312246> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_237_ArtBerman ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-10-09 -- the three research 'Episode Foundations' cited in Frankly_161's show notes.
#############################################

tgs:Work.CanariesInTheCoalMine a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Canaries in the Coal Mine? Six Facts about the Recent Employment Effects of Artificial Intelligence"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Erik Brynjolfsson, Bharat Chandar and Ruyu Chen, Stanford Digital Economy Lab working paper, first released August 2025, revised 12 August 2026. Uses ADP administrative payroll data covering millions of US workers. Finds employment of 22- to 25-year-olds in the most AI-exposed occupations falling relative to less-exposed peers: about 13% in the original release (per Frankly_161's show notes) and 19% below trend in the 2026 revision, with data through June 2026 (verified on the Stanford page, 2026-10-09). Cited in Frankly_161 as evidence for tgs:Concept.BrokenApprenticeship."@en ;
    dct:source <https://digitaleconomy.stanford.edu/publication/canaries-in-the-coal-mine-six-facts-about-the-recent-employment-effects-of-artificial-intelligence/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_161_HumanitysIcarusMoment ;
    .

tgs:Work.GenerativeAIWithoutGuardrailsCanHarmLearning a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Generative AI Without Guardrails Can Harm Learning: Evidence from High School Mathematics"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Hamsa Bastani, Osbert Bastani, Alp Sungu, Haosen Ge, Özge Kabakcı and Rei Mariman, PNAS vol. 122 (2025), DOI 10.1073/pnas.2422633122; earlier circulated on SSRN as 'Generative AI Can Harm Learning' (Wharton explainer, August 2024). Field experiment with nearly 1,000 Turkish high-school math students: an unguarded GPT-4-style interface raised practice performance 48% but lowered unassisted exam performance 17% versus control; a tutor version with guardrails raised practice 127% with no exam penalty. Cited in Frankly_161, risk #13. Note that the guardrailed result is not mentioned in the episode."@en ;
    dct:source <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2422633122> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_161_HumanitysIcarusMoment ;
    .

tgs:Work.YourBrainOnChatGPT a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Your Brain on ChatGPT: Accumulation of Cognitive Debt when Using an AI Assistant for Essay Writing Task"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Nataliya Kosmyna, Eugene Hauptmann, Ye Tong Yuan, Jessica Situ, Xian-Hao Liao, Ashly Vivian Beresnitzky, Iris Braunstein and Pattie Maes, MIT Media Lab, arXiv 2506.08872 (v1 10 June 2025, v2 31 December 2025). 54 participants wrote essays with an LLM, a search engine, or no tools across three sessions (18 switched groups in a fourth) under EEG; LLM users showed the weakest brain connectivity and underperformed on neural, linguistic and behavioral measures. Source of the term 'cognitive debt' as Nate uses it in Frankly_161, risk #13."@en ;
    dct:source <https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.08872> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_161_HumanitysIcarusMoment ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-10-10 -- Joseph Campbell's book, source of tgs:Concept.HerosJourney.
#############################################

tgs:Work.TheHeroWithAThousandFaces a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Hero with a Thousand Faces"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Joseph Campbell, 1949 (Bollingen Series XVII). A work of comparative mythology setting out the 'monomyth' (tgs:Concept.HerosJourney): a pattern of separation, initiation and return that Campbell traced across the world's myths and religions. Later a major influence on George Lucas's Star Wars."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JosephCampbell ;
    dct:issued "1949"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <http://dbpedia.org/resource/The_Hero_with_a_Thousand_Faces> ;
    .

tgs:Work.AShortHistoryOfProgress a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Short History of Progress"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Ronald Wright, 2004 (Toronto: House of Anansi), his contribution to the 2004 CBC Massey Lectures. Examines civilization's roughly 10,000-year experiment through a series of 'progress traps' and concludes that civilization must become environmentally sustainable to survive. Won the 2005 CBA Libris Award for Non-Fiction Book of the Year. Nate says he often reads its Cortés passage in his presentations: independent civilizations on both sides of the Earth arrived at the same template of surplus, scale and hierarchy."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RonaldWright ;
    dct:issued "2004"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://archive.org/details/shorthistoryofpr0000wrig/mode/2up> ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Short_History_of_Progress> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_105_KeyBlindspotsOfTheWalrusMovement ;
    .

tgs:Work.AmericanMania a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "American Mania: When More Is Not Enough"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Peter C. Whybrow, 2005 (W. W. Norton). Argues that a consumer culture of constant novelty and reward pushes the brain's ancient reward systems beyond what they evolved for, producing a culture-wide mania of overwork, debt and dissatisfaction. Nate says he found Wolfram Schultz's dopamine experiments through this book (Frankly_103)."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.PeterWhybrow ;
    dct:issued "2005"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <http://www.petercwhybrow.com/american-mania.html> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_103_TheGhostOfDopaminePast ;
    .

tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Economics for the Future -- Beyond the Superorganism"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "N. J. Hagens, Ecological Economics (2020). Nate's peer-reviewed statement of the economic Superorganism: a global economy that self-organizes around growth, powered by fossil energy and directed by financial markets, and what an economics for the energy-constrained future would need to account for. Linked from Frankly_102's show notes as the reference for 'the economic Superorganism.'"@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.NateHagens ;
    dct:issued "2020"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0921800919310067> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_102_ThisWeeksLearnings, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_160_AStateOfTheWorldAddress, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency, tgs:Interview.TGS_195_RutgerBregman, tgs:Monologue.TGS_187_EarthDay2025, tgs:Interview.TGS_186_WesCarter, tgs:Interview.TGS_183_RodSchoonover, tgs:Interview.TGS_71_DanielSchmachtenberger, tgs:Interview.TGS_42_ModelingDriversMetacrisis, tgs:Interview.TGS_31_SensemakingUncertaintyPurpose, tgs:Interview.TGS_20_MaximumPowerHyperAgents, tgs:Interview.TGS_5_BendNotBreakPart1, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_85_TheLightTriad, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_54_ReflectionsFromIndia, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_73_TheSuperorganismAndTheSelf, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_81_TimeTravelAndTheSuperorganism ;
    .

tgs:Work.TinyHabits a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tiny Habits: The Small Changes That Change Everything"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "BJ Fogg, Stanford behavior scientist, 2019. A method for building habits by making the first step so small that success is nearly guaranteed and anchoring it to an existing routine. Nate says (Frankly_129) he learned about mastery experiences from it two years earlier: each small win gives the nervous system 'better proof instead of another broken promise.'"@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.BJFogg ;
    dct:issued "2019"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://tinyhabits.com/book/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheCollapseOfComplexSocieties a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Collapse of Complex Societies"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Joseph A. Tainter, 1988 (Cambridge University Press). Argues that societies solve problems by adding complexity, that complexity faces diminishing marginal returns, and that collapse is a rational simplification when its costs outrun its benefits; the basis for tgs:Concept.ComplexityCollapse. Cited by Nate in Frankly_148 on why Fortress World's surveillance and enforcement become unaffordable as energy and complexity decline ('just ask the Romans, or Joseph Tainter')."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JosephTainter ;
    dct:issued "1988"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Collapse_of_Complex_Societies> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_148_Part4CompositeWorlds, tgs:Interview.TGS_221_AndrewHolecek, tgs:Interview.TGS_209_BalazsMatics ;
    .

tgs:Work.BullshitJobs a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bullshit Jobs: A Theory"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "David Graeber, 2018 (Simon & Schuster), expanding his 2013 essay 'On the Phenomenon of Bullshit Jobs': a large share of modern paid work is pointless even to those who do it. Cited by Nate in Frankly_148: the Long Repair would have 'fewer of what David Graeber called bullshit jobs' and more respect for physical, maintenance and care work."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.DavidGraeber ;
    dct:issued "2018"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bullshit_Jobs> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_148_Part4CompositeWorlds ;
    .

tgs:Work.MansSearchForMeaning a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Man's Search for Meaning"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Viktor E. Frankl, 1946 (originally published in German). An account of his imprisonment in Nazi concentration camps and the basis of logotherapy, arguing that people retain the freedom to choose their attitude and find meaning even in conditions they cannot change. Cited by Nate in Frankly_142 as the root of his first pathway through dread, reframing."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.ViktorFrankl ;
    dct:issued "1946"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Man%27s_Search_for_Meaning> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_142_GuideToStayingHumanPart2 ;
    .

tgs:Work.AWanderingMindIsAnUnhappyMind a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Wandering Mind Is an Unhappy Mind"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Matthew A. Killingsworth and Daniel T. Gilbert (Harvard), Science 330 (2010). Experience sampling of about a quarter of a million moments via the Track Your Happiness phone app found people's minds wandering in 46.9% of samples and people less happy when their minds wandered, whatever they were doing. Cited by Nate in Frankly_143 (he rounds to 47%)."@en ;
    dct:issued "2010"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://greatergood.berkeley.edu/images/uploads/A_Wandering_Mind_Is_an_Unhappy_Mind.pdf> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_143_GuideToStayingHumanPart3 ;
    .

tgs:Work.ADefaultModeOfBrainFunction a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Default Mode of Brain Function"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Marcus E. Raichle and colleagues, Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 98 (2001). Proposed a baseline 'default mode' of brain activity in regions that deactivate during goal-directed tasks, the origin of the term default mode network (tgs:Concept.DefaultModeNetwork). Cited by Nate in Frankly_143 as the canonical paper."@en ;
    dct:issued "2001"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11209064/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_143_GuideToStayingHumanPart3 ;
    .

tgs:Work.SacramentOfThePresentMoment a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Sacrament of the Present Moment (Abandonment to Divine Providence)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Spiritual teaching attributed to the French Jesuit Jean-Pierre de Caussade (1675-1751), compiled from his letters and talks and first published in 1861 as L'Abandon a la providence divine; known in English as Abandonment to Divine Providence and, in a later translation, The Sacrament of the Present Moment. Its idea, as Nate puts it in Frankly_143: whatever is occurring now is the form in which life is being given to you, and to be elsewhere is a refusal of the gift."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JeanPierreDeCaussade ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean_Pierre_de_Caussade> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_143_GuideToStayingHumanPart3 ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheStrategyOfTheGenes a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Strategy of the Genes"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "C. H. Waddington, 1957 (George Allen & Unwin). A study of developmental biology and genetics that presents the epigenetic landscape, in which a developing cell rolls down branching valleys toward a committed fate. Cited in Frankly_145's show notes as the source of the landscape image Nate builds his civilizational terrain map on."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.ConradWaddington ;
    dct:issued "1957"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.taylorfrancis.com/books/mono/10.4324/9781315765471/strategy-genes-waddington> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_145_Part3UphillFutures ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheSenecaEffect a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Seneca Effect: Why Growth Is Slow but Collapse Is Rapid"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Ugo Bardi, 2017 (Springer), a Report to the Club of Rome. Argues that complex systems tend to decline much faster than they grew, taking the name from Seneca's remark that fortune grows slowly but ruin is swift. Cited in Frankly_145, where Nate credits his 'friend in Italy' for the asymmetry between slow-built, high-trust systems and their rapid collapse."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.UgoBardi ;
    dct:issued "2017"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.clubofrome.org/publication/the-seneca-effect-2017/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_145_Part3UphillFutures ;
    .

tgs:Work.BombingToWin a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bombing to Win: Air Power and Coercion in War"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Robert A. Pape, 1996 (Cornell University Press). A study of when strategic bombing coerces an enemy, comparing punishment, risk, denial and decapitation strategies. Cited by Pape in TGS_235 as the source of his analysis of punishment strategies and of the British plans to firebomb 58 German towns."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RobertPape ;
    dct:issued "1996"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://cpost.uchicago.edu/publications/bombing_to_win_air_power_and_coercion_in_war> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_235_RobertPape ;
    .

tgs:Work.DyingToWin a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dying to Win: The Strategic Logic of Suicide Terrorism"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Robert A. Pape, 2005 (Random House). Argues from a database of suicide attacks that suicide terrorism is mainly a strategic response to foreign military occupation rather than a product of religion. Linked in TGS_235's show notes where Pape insists Iran wants power, not 'a religious goal.'"@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RobertPape ;
    dct:issued "2005"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dying_to_Win> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_235_RobertPape ;
    .

tgs:Work.OurOwnWorstEnemies a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Our Own Worst Enemies: America in the Age of Violent Populism"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Robert A. Pape, published September 22, 2026 (The New Press, per the show notes' publisher link), the day before TGS_235 aired. Listed in the episode's show notes as his newest book; not discussed on air."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RobertPape ;
    dct:issued "2026-09-22"^^xsd:date ;
    owl:sameAs <https://thenewpress.org/books/our-own-worst-enemies/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_235_RobertPape ;
    .

tgs:Work.WhyEconomicSanctionsDoNotWork a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Why Economic Sanctions Do Not Work"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Robert A. Pape, International Security 22(2), 1997. Argues that economic sanctions rarely achieve major political goals. The featured article in TGS_235's show notes, linked where Pape describes the April 2026 US naval blockade of Iran as punishment of the whole society."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RobertPape ;
    dct:issued "1997"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://direct.mit.edu/isec/article/22/2/90/11579/Why-Economic-Sanctions-Do-Not-Work> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_235_RobertPape ;
    .

tgs:Work.HammerAndAnvil a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Hammer and Anvil: How to Defeat ISIS"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Foreign Affairs, January 2015, co-authored by Robert A. Pape. Proposes defeating ISIS by pairing limited US air power (the hammer) with local ground forces (the anvil). Listed among TGS_235's Episode Foundations and linked where Pape cites the ISIS campaign as a model of offshore balancing."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RobertPape ;
    dct:issued "2015"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.foreignaffairs.com/articles/iraq/2015-01-02/hammer-and-anvil> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_235_RobertPape ;
    .

tgs:Work.EscalationTrapSubstack a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Escalation Trap (Substack)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Robert Pape's running Substack analysis of the 2026 US-Iran war. TGS_235 cites two posts: 'Uncertain Deal, And Iran Certainly Enters Its Period of Maximum Leverage' (June 15, 2026), which forecast a second Red Sea front and expanded Iranian demands, and 'Iran's October Surprise' (September 14, 2026), on the prospect of an Iranian nuclear test."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RobertPape ;
    owl:sameAs <https://escalationtrap.substack.com/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_235_RobertPape ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheClimateBrink a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Climate Brink (newsletter and dashboard)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Zeke Hausfather's Substack newsletter and the associated Climate Brink Dashboard of global temperature updates, histories and projections. Listed among TGS_236's Episode Foundations; many of the episode's figures (the El Niño lag, the 2027 forecast, aerosol declines, the acceleration debate) link to its posts."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.ZekeHausfather ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.theclimatebrink.com/> ;
    owl:sameAs <https://dashboard.theclimatebrink.com/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_236_ZekeHausfather ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheRealEnergyUseOfAgenticAI a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The real energy use of agentic AI"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Zeke Hausfather, The Climate Brink, 2026. Estimates AI energy use beyond single chatbot prompts from four months of his own Claude Code use: a median session of about 0.6 kWh (range 0.25-1.2 kWh) and an average day of agent use of about 3.0 kWh, more than running two refrigerators, against about 0.24 Wh for a median Gemini text prompt. Discussed in TGS_236, where he expects Jevons paradox to push AI energy use higher."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.ZekeHausfather ;
    dct:issued "2026"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.theclimatebrink.com/p/the-real-energy-use-of-agentic-ai> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_236_ZekeHausfather ;
    .

tgs:Work.CMIP7ScenariosForAR7 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "CMIP7 scenarios for the IPCC Seventh Assessment Report (ScenarioMIP)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Geoscientific Model Development 19:2627 (2026). The ScenarioMIP paper setting out the emissions scenarios for the Seventh Assessment Report, named high, medium, low and very low and extended to 2150, with the high scenario built on an SSP3 world (about 3.3°C by 2100, range 2.5-4.4°C). Listed among TGS_236's Episode Foundations; Hausfather describes it as work 'we published a few months back.' Author list not modeled here."@en ;
    dct:issued "2026"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://gmd.copernicus.org/articles/19/2627/2026/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_236_ZekeHausfather ;
    .

tgs:Work.RecentStrengtheningOfEasternPacificENSO a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Recent strengthening of eastern Pacific ENSO in the last millennium paleorecord"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Science, 2026. Uses a thousand-year Galápagos coral record to find that El Niño events of the last 50 years are stronger than any earlier in the record. Listed among TGS_236's Episode Foundations; Hausfather cites it on air as 'published in Nature a couple weeks back' (the show notes correct the journal) while cautioning against over-attributing El Niño strength to climate change. Author list not modeled here."@en ;
    dct:issued "2026"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.ady2660> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_236_ZekeHausfather ;
    .

tgs:Work.EcologicalEconomicsPrinciplesAndApplications a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ecological Economics: Principles and Applications"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Herman E. Daly and Joshua Farley, Island Press, 2003 (2nd edition 2010). A foundational textbook of ecological economics, treating the economy as a subsystem of a finite biosphere. Named in Farley's TGS_185 guest bio; on air he says he is using his sabbatical to revise 'the ecological economics textbook' substantially."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.HermanDaly, tgs:Human.JoshFarley ;
    dct:issued "2003"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_185_MythsShapingOurEconomies ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheSabbath a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Sabbath: Its Meaning for Modern Man"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Abraham Joshua Heschel, 1951. A meditation on the Sabbath as 'a palace in time.' In TGS_217 Iain McGilchrist recommends it and credits Heschel with the distinction between halakha and aggadah that he maps onto the left and right hemispheres. Heschel is not yet modeled as a Human or Persona."@en ;
    dct:issued "1951"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Sabbath_(Heschel)> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_217_WisdomInAWorldInCrisis ;
    .

tgs:Work.WholenessAndTheImplicateOrder a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wholeness and the Implicate Order"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "David Bohm, 1980. The physicist's account of an undivided wholeness in which explicit, unfolded order emerges from and returns to an implicate, enfolded order. In TGS_217 Iain McGilchrist says it 'meant an enormous amount' to him, as an image of the implicit being made explicit without destroying the whole. Bohm is not yet modeled as a Human or Persona."@en ;
    dct:issued "1980"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wholeness_and_the_Implicate_Order> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_217_WisdomInAWorldInCrisis, tgs:Interview.TGS_31_SensemakingUncertaintyPurpose ;
    .

tgs:Work.LivingInWonder a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Living in Wonder"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Rod Dreher, 2024 (full title: Living in Wonder: Finding Mystery and Meaning in a Secular Age). Cited by Iain McGilchrist in TGS_217 for the loss of wonder he attributes to reductive materialism; wonder is one of the three qualities, with humility and compassion, he expects in a reoriented person. Dreher is not yet modeled as a Human or Persona."@en ;
    dct:issued "2024"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_217_WisdomInAWorldInCrisis ;
    .

tgs:Work.TotalEclipseOfTheMind a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Total Eclipse of the Mind: Unleashing the Power of Darkness for Creativity, Healing, and Transformation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Andrew Holecek, published May 2026, drawing on more than thirty years of his own dark retreats, with a foreword by Tenzin Wangyal Rinpoche. Discussed in TGS_221; Holecek says he wrote it as much to keep unprepared people out of dark retreat as to invite prepared ones in, and it includes a chapter on contraindications developed with physicians, psychologists, psychiatrists and ophthalmologists."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.AndrewHolecek ;
    dct:issued "2026"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.andrewholecek.com/total-eclipse-of-the-mind-book/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_221_AndrewHolecek ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheHonestSorcerer a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Honest Sorcerer (Substack)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Balázs Matics's Substack blog on energy, economics, industrial materials and the future of civilization, written anonymously until his TGS_209 interview. Essays cited in that episode's show notes include 'Beyond the Mordor Economy', 'Life in the Empty Quarter', '2026: Year of the [Wile E.] Coyote', 'A Diesel-Powered Civilization', 'The Oil Import Curse', 'The Spectre of Peak Oil', 'Running on Empty: Copper' and 'Peak Steel'; TGS_220's show notes also link two of its posts on diesel and the 'next economy'."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.BalazsMatics ;
    owl:sameAs <https://thehonestsorcerer.substack.com/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_209_BalazsMatics ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheMinistryForTheFuture a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Ministry for the Future"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "2020 climate novel by Kim Stanley Robinson (Orbit Books), the subject of his TGS_66 appearance. In TGS_204 Kelly Erhart cites its glaciologist character who pumps meltwater from beneath Antarctic glaciers to slow them, as an example of a glacier-stabilization intervention."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Ministry_for_the_Future> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_204_KellyErhart, tgs:Interview.TGS_200_TedParson, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_54_ReflectionsFromIndia ;
    .

tgs:Work.AFewRulesForPredictingTheFuture a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Few Rules for Predicting the Future"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Essay by the novelist Octavia E. Butler (Essence, 2000). Kelly Erhart, calling it 'Seven Lessons for Predicting the Future' in TGS_204, cites Butler's memory of daily Cold War bomb drills, when no one could imagine the decades of relative peace that followed, as her reason for wanting children despite a disrupted future."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://antiableistcomposition.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/octavia_e._butler_a_few_rules_.pdf> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_204_KellyErhart ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheSpiritOfTheRainforest a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Spirit of the Rainforest: How Indigenous Wisdom and Scientific Curiosity Reconnects Us to the Natural World"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Book by Rosa Vásquez Espinoza (2025), an adventure-driven account of Amazonian wildlife, places and stories and of her work joining Indigenous knowledge and science; one of its last chapters describes the ayahuasca experience that led her to study animal self-medication. Promoted in TGS_201, where Nate calls it 'the Spirit in the Forest'."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RosaVasquezEspinoza ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.rosavespinoza.com/book> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_201_RosaVasquezEspinoza ;
    .

tgs:Work.SolarGeoengineeringHistoryMethodsGovernanceProspects a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Solar Geoengineering: History, Methods, Governance, Prospects"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Review article by Edward A. (Ted) Parson and David W. Keith in the Annual Review of Environment and Resources, covering the history, methods, governance and prospects of solar geoengineering. Linked in the show notes of both TGS_200 and TGS_204."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.TedParson ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.annualreviews.org/content/journals/10.1146/annurev-environ-112321-081911> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_200_TedParson, tgs:Interview.TGS_204_KellyErhart ;
    .

tgs:Work.SolarGeoengineeringScenariosOfFutureGovernanceChallenges a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Solar geoengineering: Scenarios of future governance challenges"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Article by Edward A. Parson and Jesse L. Reynolds in Futures (2021), using scenarios to explore governance challenges that solar geoengineering could raise; the basis for the political scenario exercises Parson describes in TGS_200. Linked in the show notes of both TGS_200 and TGS_204."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.TedParson ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0016328721001154> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_200_TedParson, tgs:Interview.TGS_204_KellyErhart ;
    .

tgs:Work.ProtectingTheOzoneLayer a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Protecting the Ozone Layer: Science and Strategy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "2003 book by Edward A. Parson (Oxford University Press), winner of the International Studies Association's Sprout Award and widely regarded as the authoritative account of how international cooperation to protect the ozone layer developed. In TGS_200 Parson calls the Montreal Protocol 'an extraordinary triumph of cooperative, scientifically informed international governance' that still works."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.TedParson ;
    owl:sameAs <https://academic.oup.com/book/32484> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_200_TedParson ;
    .

tgs:Work.TurnsOutAirPollutionWasGoodForSomething a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Turns Out Air Pollution Was Good for Something"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "New York Times opinion essay by Zeke Hausfather and David Keith (2025-09-21) on the cooling that sulfur pollution provided and the case for considering a small stratospheric replacement as it is cleaned up. In TGS_200, recorded four days later, Ted Parson cites it as an op-ed by colleagues 'a couple of days ago': for every hundred units of sulfur cut near the ground, perhaps one could go up high."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.ZekeHausfather ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.nytimes.com/2025/09/21/opinion/geoengineering-planet-cooling-sulfur.html> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_200_TedParson ;
    .

tgs:Work.MoralAmbition a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Moral Ambition: Stop Wasting Your Talent and Start Making a Difference"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Book by Rutger Bregman (2025), the subject of TGS_195, which he describes as partly a self-help book written for himself. It draws on the British abolitionists, the suffragists, the move from the Gilded Age to the Progressive Era, resistance rescuers in the Second World War and the Shrimp Welfare Project; all his income from it goes to the School for Moral Ambition."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RutgerBregman ;
    owl:sameAs <https://rutgerbregman.com/books> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_195_RutgerBregman ;
    .

tgs:Work.Humankind a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Humankind: A Hopeful History"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Book by Rutger Bregman (2020), a Sunday Times and New York Times bestseller arguing for fundamental human decency and 'survival of the friendliest'; its unexpected success led him to take the Giving What We Can pledge, and its argument underlies the kindness principle of the School for Moral Ambition (TGS_195)."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RutgerBregman ;
    owl:sameAs <https://rutgerbregman.com/books> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_195_RutgerBregman ;
    .

tgs:Work.UtopiaForRealists a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Utopia for Realists: And How We Can Get There"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Book by Rutger Bregman (English edition 2017), a Sunday Times and New York Times bestseller; in TGS_195 he recalls its chapter on moving beyond GDP as a measure of wealth."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RutgerBregman ;
    owl:sameAs <https://rutgerbregman.com/books> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_195_RutgerBregman ;
    .

tgs:Work.MoralCapital a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Moral Capital: Foundations of British Abolitionism"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Book by the historian Christopher Leslie Brown (University of North Carolina Press, 2006). Rutger Bregman (TGS_195) draws on its argument that, after losing the American colonies, Britain's identity crisis let abolitionists present ending the slave trade as national redemption and part of the British character."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://uncpress.org/9780807856987/moral-capital/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_195_RutgerBregman ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheBurningEarth a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Burning Earth: A History"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Book by Sunil Amrith (W. W. Norton, 2024), an environmental history of the modern world that foregrounds the experience of the Global South, beginning about a thousand years ago with the expansion of rice cultivation in China; named a 2024 essential read by The New Yorker and a 'book we love' by NPR. Its chapter on migration opens with a quotation from Chekhov's Sakhalin Island (TGS_192)."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.SunilAmrith ;
    owl:sameAs <https://wwnorton.com/books/9781324007180> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_192_SunilAmrith ;
    .

tgs:Work.UnrulyWaters a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Unruly Waters: How Rains, Rivers, Coasts, and Seas Have Shaped Asia's History"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Book by Sunil Amrith (Basic Books, 2018) on the relationship between water and human societies in Asia, including violent conflict, inequality and migration; discussed in TGS_192."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.SunilAmrith ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.hachettebookgroup.com/titles/sunil-amrith/unruly-waters/9780465097739/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_192_SunilAmrith ;
    .

tgs:Work.CrossingTheBayOfBengal a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Crossing the Bay of Bengal: The Furies of Nature and the Fortunes of Migrants"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Book by Sunil Amrith (Harvard University Press, 2013) on centuries of migration and cultural exchange around the Bay of Bengal, the starting point of his view, presented in TGS_192, that migration is a normal part of human life."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.SunilAmrith ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_192_SunilAmrith ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheInsectCrisis a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Insect Crisis"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Oliver Milman's first book (Atlantic Books, 2022), an account of the worldwide collapse of insect populations and its threat to everything from birds to food; shortlisted for the Wainwright Prize for Conservation Writing. Its reporting includes the monarch butterfly overwintering forests of central Mexico. The subject of TGS_189."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.OliverMilman ;
    owl:sameAs <https://olivermilman.com/the-insect-crisis/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_189_OliverMilman ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheLittleThingsThatRunTheWorld a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Little Things That Run the World (The Importance and Conservation of Invertebrates)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "1987 essay by E. O. Wilson in Conservation Biology arguing that invertebrates, insects above all, sustain ecosystems and deserve conservation attention. Oliver Milman borrows its title phrase in TGS_189."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.EOWilson ;
    owl:sameAs <https://faculty.washington.edu/timbillo/Readings%20and%20documents/ABRIDGED%20READINGS%20for%20PERU/Wilson_1987_Little_things_that_run.pdf> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_189_OliverMilman ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheSovereignIndividual a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Sovereign Individual"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "1997 book by James Dale Davidson and William Rees-Mogg predicting that digital technology and cybercurrency would let some individuals escape the power of nation states. In TGS_187 Nate credits it with being 'somewhat right' and uses it as the foil for his wider-boundary idea of sovereignty."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Sovereign_Individual> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.TGS_187_EarthDay2025, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_82_PowerVsLife ;
    .

tgs:Work.ClimateChangeNationalIntelligenceAssessment2008 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "2008 Intelligence Community Assessment of the National Security Implications of Global Climate Change (classified)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The US intelligence community's first flagship assessment of climate change's national security implications, produced in 2008 under President George W. Bush, signed off by every intelligence agency and welcomed across parties; it remains classified although, per Rod Schoonover (TGS_183), it contains 'practically nothing classified'. Schoonover has pushed for its declassification; the show notes link the National Security Archive's Climate Change Transparency Project."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://nsarchive.gwu.edu/briefing-book/climate-change-transparency-project-intelligence/2025-02-28/climate-intelligence> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_183_RodSchoonover ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheSecurityThreatThatBindsUs a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Security Threat That Binds Us: The Unraveling of Ecological and Natural Security and What the United States Can Do About It"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "2021 report from the Council on Strategic Risks, with Rod Schoonover among its authors, on ecological disruption as a national security threat and US policy responses; linked in the TGS_183 show notes."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RodSchoonover ;
    owl:sameAs <https://councilonstrategicrisks.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/The-Security-Threat-That-Binds-Us_2021_2-1.pdf> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_183_RodSchoonover ;
    .

tgs:Work.FiveUrgentQuestionsOnEcologicalSecurity a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Five Urgent Questions on Ecological Security"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "SIPRI Insights on Peace and Security paper (2023) by Rod Schoonover and colleagues, setting out the research and policy agenda for ecological security; linked in the TGS_183 show notes."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RodSchoonover ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.sipri.org/publications/2023/sipri-insights-peace-and-security/five-urgent-questions-ecological-security> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_183_RodSchoonover ;
    .

tgs:Work.CivilizationalOptionalityEssay a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Civilizational Optionality"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Essay by Indy Johar published by the Long Now Foundation, accompanying his Long Now talk 'Civilizational Optioneering' (2026). Listed under Episode Foundations in the TGS_238 show notes."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.IndyJohar ;
    owl:sameAs <https://longnow.org/ideas/civilizational-optionality-indy-johar-long-now/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_238_IndyJohar ;
    .

tgs:Work.MoneyAsAClaimOnFutureEnergy a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Money as a Claim on Future Energy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Substack essay by Indy Johar, listed under Episode Foundations in the TGS_238 show notes; its title echoes Nate's own framing (tgs:Concept.MoneyAsClaimOnEnergy)."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.IndyJohar ;
    owl:sameAs <https://indyjohar.substack.com/p/money-as-a-claim-on-future-energy> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_238_IndyJohar ;
    .

tgs:Work.LoadingTheDiceAgainstPensions a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Loading the DICE Against Pensions"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Carbon Tracker report on how climate-economy models such as DICE, used by pension funds, understate climate risk. Listed under Next Steps in the TGS_238 show notes."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://carbontracker.org/reports/loading-the-dice-against-pensions/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_238_IndyJohar ;
    .

tgs:Work.GlobalPolycrisisCausalMechanisms a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Global polycrisis: the causal mechanisms of crisis entanglement"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Research article in Global Sustainability (Cambridge University Press) on how crises interact and amplify one another; linked twice in the TGS_238 show notes, on coupled, non-linear systems and on coupled risks."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/global-sustainability/article/global-polycrisis-the-causal-mechanisms-of-crisis-entanglement/06F0F8F3B993A221971151E3CB054B5E> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_238_IndyJohar ;
    .

tgs:Work.SeeingLikeAState a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Seeing Like a State"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "James C. Scott's 1998 book on how states simplify landscapes and societies to make them legible, and why such schemes fail. Indy Johar cites it in TGS_238 on the simplification behind industrial agriculture and machines."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seeing_Like_a_State> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_238_IndyJohar ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheCodeOfCapital a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Code of Capital: How the Law Creates Wealth and Inequality"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Book by Katharina Pistor (Princeton University Press, 2019) arguing that capital is created by legally coding assets through property, contract, trust, corporate and bankruptcy law. Indy Johar cites it in TGS_147 for the claim that the coding of capital is rooted in theories of property."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://press.princeton.edu/books/hardcover/9780691178974/the-code-of-capital> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_147_IndyJohar ;
    .

tgs:Work.Novacene a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Novacene: The Coming Age of Hyperintelligence"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "James Lovelock's 2019 book. Indy Johar praises its first third in TGS_147 for narrating the Earth's stored solar energy being spent to give birth to a self-aware planetary consciousness, and returns to Lovelock in TGS_238."@en ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.amazon.com/Novacene-Coming-Hyperintelligence-James-Lovelock/dp/024139936X> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_147_IndyJohar ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheCalculusOfConsent a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Calculus of Consent: Logical Foundations of Constitutional Democracy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "1962 book by James M. Buchanan and Gordon Tullock, a founding text of public choice theory (tgs:Concept.PublicChoiceTheory). Treats political actors as the same self-interested individuals found in markets and focuses on the rules under which collective decisions are made. The source of Frankly_162's rules rung; a viewer suggested it to Nate."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.econlib.org/library/Buchanan/buchCv3.html> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_162_SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem ;
    .

tgs:Work.LeveragePointsPlacesToIntervene a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Leverage Points: Places to Intervene in a System"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Essay by Donella Meadows (Sustainability Institute, 1999) ranking twelve places to intervene in a complex system, from parameters at the shallow end to paradigms and the power to transcend them at the deep end. Basis of tgs:Concept.LeveragePoints; cited in Frankly_162."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.DonellaMeadows ;
    dct:source <https://donellameadows.org/archives/leverage-points-places-to-intervene-in-a-system/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_162_SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem ;
    .

tgs:Work.NationalIncome1929To1932 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "National Income, 1929-1932"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Simon Kuznets's 1934 report to the US Senate, which established the national income accounts from which GDP became the central measure of economic success. It contains the warning, quoted in Frankly_162, that the welfare of a nation can scarcely be inferred from a measurement of national income -- the designer flagging the metric's misuse in advance."@en ;
    dct:source <https://fraser.stlouisfed.org/files/docs/publications/natincome_1934/19340104_nationalinc.pdf> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_162_SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem ;
    .

tgs:Work.HierarchyInTheForest a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Hierarchy in the Forest: The Evolution of Egalitarian Behavior"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "1999 book by anthropologist Christopher Boehm (Harvard University Press) arguing that egalitarian hunter-gatherer bands were maintained by a reverse dominance hierarchy, in which the group collectively restrained would-be dominants. Basis of tgs:Concept.ReverseDominanceHierarchy; cited in Frankly_162's show notes."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.hup.harvard.edu/books/9780674006911> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_162_SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem ;
    .

tgs:Work.TacklingClimateChangeWithMachineLearning a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tackling Climate Change with Machine Learning"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Survey paper by David Rolnick and colleagues, published in ACM Computing Surveys (2022), cataloguing ways machine learning could help reduce emissions and adapt to climate change. Cited by Daniel Schmachtenberger in TGS_132 as the most comprehensive statement of AI's claimed climate benefits, which he then assesses as mostly marginal efficiency gains."@en ;
    dct:source <https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3485128> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_132_WideBoundaryImpactsOfAI ;
    .

tgs:Work.DevelopmentInProgress a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Development in Progress"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Consilience Project paper in two parts -- Part I, 'Seeing the Bigger Picture,' and Part II, 'Progress as Holistic Betterment' -- arguing that the prevailing idea of progress is 'immature' and 'developmentally incomplete' because it ignores or downplays the side effects of technologies, with leaded gasoline and Haber-Bosch fertilizer as worked examples, and that those harms grow with technological power and scale. A mature progress would 'systematically identify and internalize its externalities' before deployment, so that changes remain good once all perspectives and effects are considered: 'Real progress would require internalizing externalities, binding social traps, and rethinking our approach to problem-solving, advancement, and technology more generally.' Its proposals include pre-release testing and risk assessment, broader metrics of progress (health, ecosystems, well-being), redesigned social media (bridging content, slower infinite scroll, downranking AI-altered content), regenerative agriculture, and caution with powerful technologies framed as pro-humanity rather than anti-progress. 'It isn't possible to practice real, authentic progress in the presence of the fake, immature version.' The subject of TGS_126 (before publication; Nate had read a draft) and linked in TGS_132's show notes as the 'Paper on Naive Progress.' Modeled in this graph as tgs:Concept.NaiveProgress and tgs:Concept.AuthenticProgress; its three moves correspond to tgs:Concept.YellowTeaming and tgs:Concept.HolisticExternalityAnalysis (internalizing externalities), tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap and tgs:Concept.RestraintByDesign (binding social traps), and tgs:Concept.ProblemSolvingAsRacket (rethinking problem-solving)."@en ;
    dct:issued "2024-07-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:abstract "The concept of progress is at the heart of humanity's story."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Enriched 2026-10-18 from the published page, confirmed by MJSullivan, who supplied the date shown at the top of the article (Jul 16, 2024) with its standfirst. The page shows no author credits; TGS_126 says it was multi-authored by Daniel Schmachtenberger's team. Publication falls after TGS_126 (recorded 2024-05-05, issued 2024-06-05), which therefore does not list it in dct:references, and one day before TGS_132 (issued 2024-07-17), whose dct:references to it is chronologically valid. Yellow teaming, the Sabbath and the Pacific Northwest fishing rules discussed in TGS_126 do not appear in the published text."@en ;
    dct:publisher tgs:Organization.ConsilienceProject ;
    dct:source <https://consilienceproject.org/development-in-progress/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_132_WideBoundaryImpactsOfAI ;
    .

tgs:Work.WhereArgumentsComeFrom a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Where Arguments Come From"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Consilience Project paper on how arguments circulate in public life: not by natural selection of good ideas but because whoever has marketing and research budgets upregulates the ideas that serve them (the 'think tank industrial complex'). Cited by Daniel Schmachtenberger in TGS_132 to explain why the 'AI will help the climate' story dominates."@en ;
    dct:publisher tgs:Organization.ConsilienceProject ;
    dct:source <https://consilienceproject.org/where-arguments-come-from/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_132_WideBoundaryImpactsOfAI ;
    .

tgs:Work.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Technology Is Not Values Neutral: Ending the Reign of Nihilistic Design"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Consilience Project paper arguing that technologies encode values: competitive advantage makes them obligate and their use reshapes human behavior and minds. Explained by Daniel Schmachtenberger in TGS_71, whose show notes link it twice. Basis of tgs:Concept.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral."@en ;
    dct:publisher tgs:Organization.ConsilienceProject ;
    dct:source <https://consilienceproject.org/technology-is-not-values-neutral-ending-the-reign-of-nihilistic-design-2/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_71_DanielSchmachtenberger, tgs:Interview.TGS_20_MaximumPowerHyperAgents, tgs:Interview.TGS_126_AVisionForBetterment ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheOverspentAmerican a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Overspent American: Why We Want What We Don't Need"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "1998 book by sociologist Juliet Schor on consumer aspiration and spending. Cited by Nate in TGS_50 (as a friend's book, 'like 30 years ago') for the finding that people, whatever they earn, want to earn more the following year -- in support of Daniel's advice not to create an embedded growth obligation on one's own earnings."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.harpercollins.com/products/the-overspent-american-juliet-b-schor?variant=32154061406242> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_50_CriteriaCategoriesResponse ;
    .

tgs:Work.Nonzero a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nonzero: The Logic of Human Destiny"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "2000 book by journalist Robert Wright (not to be confused with tgs:Persona.RonaldWright) arguing that cultural and biological evolution tend toward ever more non-zero-sum, cooperative interaction at ever larger scales. Cited by Daniel Schmachtenberger in TGS_42, who accepts the in-group trend but adds that it was zero- or negative-sum between groups and negative-sum to nature (tgs:Concept.PositiveSumInGroupNegativeSumToNature)."@en ;
    dct:issued "2000"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_42_ModelingDriversMetacrisis ;
    .

tgs:Work.DietForANewAmerica a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Diet for a New America"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "1987 book by John Robbins on the health, ethical and environmental costs of industrial animal agriculture. Daniel Schmachtenberger says in TGS_31 that he read it the day after looking into a cattle truck bound for slaughter as a child, the day he stopped eating animals; his concern later widened from factory farms to systemic risk."@en ;
    dct:issued "1987"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://www.amazon.com/Diet-New-America-Choices-Happiness/dp/193207354X> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_31_SensemakingUncertaintyPurpose ;
    .

tgs:Work.TragedyOfTheCommons1968 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Tragedy of the Commons"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Garrett Hardin's 1968 essay in Science (vol. 162), arguing that freely accessible shared resources are overexploited unless use is restrained, and that some problems have no technical solution. Linked in the TGS_50 show notes at Daniel Schmachtenberger's discussion of why harms get externalized. Basis of tgs:Concept.TragedyOfTheCommons."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.GarrettHardin ;
    dct:issued "1968"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://www.jstor.org/stable/1724745> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_50_CriteriaCategoriesResponse ;
    .

tgs:Work.SilentSpring a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Silent Spring"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Rachel Carson's 1962 book documenting the ecological harm of synthetic pesticides, especially DDT, widely credited with launching the modern environmental movement. Cited by Daniel Schmachtenberger in TGS_126 as the record of 'better living through chemistry' -- narrow technological success with vast externalities."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RachelCarson ;
    dct:issued "1962"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <http://www.rachelcarson.org/SilentSpring.aspx> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_126_AVisionForBetterment ;
    .

tgs:Work.WarIsARacket a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "War Is a Racket"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "1935 speech and short book by retired US Marine Corps Major General Smedley Butler, arguing that war is 'possibly the oldest, easily the most profitable, surely the most vicious' racket, conducted for the benefit of a few at the expense of many. Daniel Schmachtenberger reads its opening in TGS_126 to define a racket as manufacturing a demand and then selling the supply (tgs:Concept.ProblemSolvingAsRacket)."@en ;
    dct:issued "1935"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_Is_a_Racket> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_126_AVisionForBetterment ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheoryOfGamesAndEconomicBehavior a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Theory of Games and Economic Behavior"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "1944 book by John von Neumann and Oskar Morgenstern that founded modern game theory and applied it to economics. Daniel Schmachtenberger cites it in TGS_126 (as von Neumann's book on economics and game theory) for the moment game theory became the most scientifically congruent answer to what a good choice is (tgs:Concept.GameTheoryFillsTheOughtGap). The show notes link a Stanford page on von Neumann and game theory."@en ;
    dct:issued "1944"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_126_AVisionForBetterment ;
    .

tgs:Work.HowToMisleadWithFacts a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "How to Mislead with Facts"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Consilience Project paper on how true facts are used to support specious conclusions: cherry-picking, decontextualizing and framing. Daniel Schmachtenberger cites it in TGS_126 when describing how progress narratives are built (tgs:Concept.ProgressNarrativeAsApologism)."@en ;
    dct:publisher tgs:Organization.ConsilienceProject ;
    dct:source <https://consilienceproject.org/how-to-mislead-the-facts/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_126_AVisionForBetterment ;
    .

tgs:Work.HospicingModernity a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Hospicing Modernity: Facing Humanity's Wrongs and the Implications for Social Activism"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "2021 book by Vanessa Machado de Oliveira (Vanessa Andreotti). In TGS_126 Daniel Schmachtenberger cites 'her book' for her chief's definition of colonialism: not fundamentally taking land or abusing people, which are epiphenomena, but believing there are separable things. Her own TGS conversation is tgs:Interview.TGS_125_VanessaAndreotti."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.VanessaAndreotti ;
    dct:issued "2021"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_126_AVisionForBetterment ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheDayAfter1983 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Day After"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "1983 American television film directed by Nicholas Meyer, broadcast by ABC on 20 November 1983, depicting nuclear war and its aftermath in Kansas and Missouri; watched by roughly 100 million Americans on its first airing. In Frankly_81 Nate cites it as a cultural intervention that catalyzed the nuclear-disarmament movement and moved President Reagan, and argues no single broadcast could reach whole nations at once in today's fractured media (tgs:Concept.SharedBroadcastPerturbation)."@en ;
    dct:issued "1983-11-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Day_After> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_81_TimeTravelAndTheSuperorganism ;
    .
